     1	# Changelog
     2	
     3	All notable changes to **ceo-orchestration** are documented in this file.
     4	
     5	The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
     6	and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).
     7	
     8	> **Scope.** This log records *user-visible* changes — new skills, hooks, slash
     9	> commands, schema/contract changes, and behavior an adopter would notice after
    10	> installing or upgrading the framework. Internal refactors, test-only churn, and
    11	> release-engineering bookkeeping are omitted. Counts cited below (as of
    12	> v1.3.0: 166 skills, 27 slash commands, 190 ADRs, 68 `_lib` modules) are
    13	> reproducible from the repository via
    14	> `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`.
    15	
    16	---
    17	
    18	## [1.3.0] - 2026-08-04
    19	
    20	Night-mode + doctrine + release-engineering train (PLAN-162/165
    21	ceremony 2; PLAN-166/167/168 release-hold closure and install/upgrade
    22	ownership). One user-facing feature, one cross-rail security P0 closed,
    23	one published-contract conflict settled by ADR instead of by silence,
    24	and the install/upgrade ownership decision collapsed into a single
    25	audited function. As always: governance and auditability — no speed
    26	claim.
    27	
    28	### Added — night-mode: Owner-invoked autonomy posture toggle (PLAN-165, ADR-185)
    29	
    30	- **`/night-mode` command + `.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`**: the Owner
    31	  flips the per-machine autonomy posture (`permissions.defaultMode`
    32	  overlay in the gitignored `.claude/settings.local.json`) with
    33	  next-session semantics — "arm before sleep, autonomous overnight
    34	  session, disarm in the morning". The tracked `settings.json` posture is
    35	  never touched; templates and adopter defaults are unchanged.
    36	- **Arming autonomy is a HUMAN action** (Owner-ratified OQ1-redo): the
    37	  toggle refuses under CI; the writer script carries a self-path guard
    38	  and the Bash rail carries a best-effort invocation matcher (defense in
    39	  depth — the real boundary is the script's own guard plus the session
    40	  deny surface, and the docs say exactly that, not more).
    41	- **Audited**: every `on`/`off`/refusal emits `night_mode_toggled` into
    42	  the HMAC chain (schema row in `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`); live-fire
    43	  proven — toggle events and a negative-control `refused` line verified
    44	  on-chain before this release.
    45	- `bypassPermissions` was deliberately CUT from the design: it would trip
    46	  the `settings_tamper_tripwires` boot check by construction. The honest
    47	  escape valve stays `claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions` (one
    48	  session, explicit, ephemeral).
    49	
    50	### Security — sentinel unlock now requires provenance inside git worktrees (ADR-119 Invariant 5)
    51	
    52	- **Adopter-visible tightening of the recovery contract** (SPEC/v1
    53	  `sentinel-format.schema.md` §unlock): inside a git work tree, the
    54	  `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK` + `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_ACK` pair alone no longer
    55	  grants — you must also provide provenance via
    56	  `CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` (captured BEFORE the
    57	  session) **or** `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256=<sha256 of approved.md>`.
    58	  The block message teaches both forms when a window is armed. This
    59	  closes a real escalation: during an unlock window the agent could
    60	  author its own `approved.md` (a path guarded by neither rail) and
    61	  self-grant. Classified MINOR under this repo's published policy
    62	  (`VERSIONING.md` §MINOR: "a new trust boundary adopters must
    63	  understand"); MAJOR is reserved for schema-consumer breakage
    64	  (removed/renamed fields, dropped event types), which this is not.
    65	  **Action needed only if you scripted the 2-var unlock inside a git
    66	  worktree** — add one of the two exports above.
    67	
    68	### Fixed — case-fold bypass on canonical AND kernel rails (PLAN-162 W2, P0)
    69	
    70	- On case-insensitive filesystems (APFS default), `fnmatch.fnmatchcase`
    71	  let `.claude/settings.JSON` / `.claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.PY` slip
    72	  past BOTH guard rails while the write lands on the real file. Both
    73	  rails now case-fold before matching. Found first-hand during the
    74	  PLAN-162 debate; verified by red-first tests on both rails.
    75	
    76	### Changed — hook-deadline doctrine settled by ADR-186 (fail-CLOSED)
    77	
    78	- The canonical-edit matcher now enforces a **per-invocation wall
    79	  deadline that fails CLOSED** (`_HOOK_WALL_BUDGET_S`, injectable clock),
    80	  replacing the unbounded sentinel sweep — and the published
    81	  fail-open-on-infrastructure contract in `CLAUDE.md` §4 / `AGENTS.md` §1
    82	  now names this exception explicitly: a timeout *inside the matcher* is
    83	  an incomplete verification, not infrastructure. Recovery route:
    84	  provenance-pinned unlock (`CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK` +
    85	  `CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA` or `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256`).
    86	- Sentinel-verification **cache partitioned** (signature validity no
    87	  longer keyed per-target). This closes the measured saturation window —
    88	  4.16 s consumed of a 5 s budget at 20 candidate paths — that made the
    89	  old fail-open deadline attacker-reachable by planting sentinels
    90	  (ADR-164-AMEND-1). A correctness/security fix; no throughput claim.
    91	- Deadline blocks are **countable in the chain**: the veto breadcrumb
    92	  carries `reason_code=canonical_edit_hook_fault` instead of
    93	  masquerading as a missing-sentinel block.
    94	
    95	### Changed — pair-rail recalibrated 120/150 → 180/210 (ADR-110-AMEND-2)
    96	
    97	- Internal cap 180 s under a 210 s registration, ratified only after a
    98	  live substrate probe proved the harness honors a 210 s hook
    99	  registration (evidence committed:
   100	  `.claude/plans/PLAN-162/probe-210s-GO-EVIDENCE.md`).
   101	- `pair_rail_case` events now carry `timeout_ms` (int — a float in an
   102	  HMAC-covered field silently drops the whole event), and the §3
   103	  escalation trigger is the **censoring rate**, not p95 (the p95 of a
   104	  censored sample is inestimable).
   105	
   106	### Fixed — scheduled workflows that were red without surfacing in push CI
   107	
   108	- **mutation-gate**: kill-rate now parsed from mutmut's junitxml (the old
   109	  regex parser NEVER reported a rate — historical "96.7%" came from an
   110	  inflated formula), artifact redaction inlined and fail-closed, and the
   111	  `actions/checkout` SHA re-pinned (which also cures the
   112	  supply-chain-watch drift red present since 2026-07-20).
   113	- **tournament**: stderr banner no longer merged into the cost-projection
   114	  JSON (`2>&1` unmerge).
   115	- **reality-ledger**: required labels created idempotently
   116	  (`gh label create --force`) so fresh installs cannot red on a missing
   117	  label.
   118	- **ceo-boot**: 24th Tier-S check `scheduled_workflows_red` closes the
   119	  "scheduled gate red for weeks, invisible" class — with cure-detection
   120	  (a red scheduled lane whose newest completed run across ALL trigger
   121	  events is green reports `cured_pending_cron`, not red).
   122	
   123	### Changed — install/upgrade semantics (PLAN-166/167/168)
   124	
   125	Adopter-visible behavior of `scripts/install.sh` / `scripts/upgrade.sh`
   126	changed in this release — what a v1.2.0 adopter notices when upgrading:
   127	
   128	- **Ownership is ONE decision, not a cascade (ADR-190).** On upgrade,
   129	  whether the framework owns `PROTOCOL.md`, `SPEC/v1` or
   130	  `.claude/.framework-version` is answered by a single pure decision
   131	  function (`_ownership_verdict()` in
   132	  `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`) over the observed ownership
   133	  dimensions — execution-state faults (e.g. a failed backup) stay
   134	  caller-side by design; `upgrade.sh` observes → calls → executes
   135	  instead of deciding locally. Fresh installs sit BEFORE the decision:
   136	  they record the registered delivery (the ADR-155 baseline manifest)
   137	  that upgrade's observations later read. Contract:
   138	  `docs/ownership-decision-table.md`.
   139	- **`SPEC/v1` is a forced route on upgrade.** A framework-owned
   140	  `SPEC/v1` is backed up to `.claude.bak/<timestamp>/SPEC/v1` and
   141	  replaced wholesale — a local edit is a fork of the published
   142	  compliance contract, not a customization. A pre-existing `SPEC/v1`
   143	  with no delivery record is byte-compared against the pristine SPECs
   144	  shipped at v1.2.0 and earlier: a match refreshes it, anything else is
   145	  preserved in place with a named WARNING (ADR-155-AMEND-1). Skipped on
   146	  `--ceremony user` installs.
   147	- **`.claude/.framework-version` is the version marker.** Written on
   148	  install and refreshed on framework-owned, unskipped upgrades;
   149	  `check-framework-updates.sh` and forensic triage read it MARKER-FIRST
   150	  — a well-formed marker validated against its delivery record is the
   151	  signal; an absent, unrecorded, malformed or integrity-failed marker
   152	  falls back to root `VERSION`. The adopter repo's root `VERSION` file
   153	  is deliberately never touched by upgrade — after a framework-owned
   154	  upgrade, the validated marker (not root `VERSION`) is what carries
   155	  the installed framework version.
   156	- **`PROTOCOL.md` pointer has ONE generator.** Install and upgrade
   157	  render the pointer through the same shared generator (byte-identical
   158	  output on both paths); a degraded pointer body is cured with a backup
   159	  and adopter edits are preserved. Skipped on `--ceremony user`
   160	  installs, which never create root files.
   161	
   162	### Governance
   163	
   164	- ADRs 184 → **190** (ADR-185 night-mode; ADR-186 hook-deadline policy;
   165	  ADR-110-AMEND-2; ADR-164-AMEND-1; ADR-155-AMEND-1 delivery-record
   166	  ownership; ADR-190 ownership-decision-table contract). Slash
   167	  commands 26 → **27**
   168	  (`/night-mode`). All four ceremony phases landed as separable
   169	  Owner-GPG-signed commits with per-phase sentinels and closed scopes.
   170	
   171	## [1.2.0] - 2026-07-30
   172	
   173	Substrate + rail release (PLAN-160/161/163/164). The headline is not a new
   174	feature: it is that the cross-model pair-rail **completed a live in-hook
   175	review for the first time in the audit log's history**. Everything else is
   176	the substrate work that made that possible. As always: governance and
   177	auditability — no speed claim.
   178	
   179	### Fixed — the pair-rail was 100% fail-open (PLAN-164, ADR-110-AMEND-1)
   180	
   181	- **`CEO_PAIR_RAIL_TIMEOUT_S` internal default 30 → 120 s**, and the
   182	  `check_pair_rail.py` PreToolUse **registration timeout 60 → 150 s** in both
   183	  kernel `.claude/settings.json` and `templates/settings/settings.base.json`
   184	  (parity enforced). The 30 s value was an implementation literal, never a
   185	  decided one, and it sat structurally *below* the latency of a real Codex
   186	  verdict: **every one of the 12 `pair_rail_case` events in the entire life of
   187	  the audit log was case F / TIMEOUT.** The rail had never once completed a
   188	  live review. Measured calibration (N=9, same machine): p95 ≈ 75 s, which
   189	  crosses the measurement protocol's own 70 s escalation threshold and selects
   190	  120/150 rather than the 100/120 first draft.
    25	- [ ] `bash .claude/scripts/validate-governance.sh` exit 0
    26	- [ ] `bash .claude/scripts/check-contamination.sh` exit 0
