docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:77:**Asset:** Claude session integrity. The next Claude turn after a Codex MCP review consumes Codex's stdout as part of its context.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:121:**Asset:** Audit log integrity (LLM06 side-channel guard). The audit log is forensic-only; it MUST NOT persist secrets that could be exfiltrated via log tap, dashboard export, or CI artifact.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:206:**Residual risk:** A simultaneous compromise of (a) the canonical-guard sentinel mechanism, (b) the kernel-override env vars, AND (c) the audit-log integrity (HMAC chain) would defeat all 6 mitigations. This is the same residual-risk class as direct edits to `_lib/audit_emit.py` itself; out-of-scope for T-4 specifically. Owner physical-time + GPG-key + HMAC-chain compromise is the threshold class assumed beyond Pair-Rail's defense surface.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:210:- `audit-query.py search 'dispatcher_route' (advisory rollup; dedicated dispatcher-routes-summary sub-command is ⏳ Phase 7 follow-up)` (Phase 6 v1.0 deliverable) reports archetype distribution + matrix_sha256_match rate + reason_code breakdown. Spike in `reason_code: matrix_sha_mismatch` → investigate matrix integrity. Drop of any archetype to 0 dispatches in active session → archetype-omission attack candidate.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:296:**Asset:** Framework canonical-path integrity. When Codex acts as `coder` (Phase 5 flipped role — Codex-as-coder, Claude-as-reviewer), Codex MCP has write-capable tool calls that could target canonical-guarded paths (e.g. `_lib/audit_emit.py`, `check_canonical_edit.py`, `SPEC/v1/`). A successful T-7 bypass allows Codex to overwrite governance-critical files without sentinel authorization.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:330:**Asset:** Codex CLI binary integrity at invocation time. The Pair-Rail architecture shells out to the Codex CLI binary via `subprocess.run(["codex", ...])`. If the binary resolved at invocation time is not the vetted version, the entire cross-LLM review surface is compromised.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:349:1. **`.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json`** ✅ **SHIPPED ADR-182** — per-targetTriple SHA-256 pin of the NATIVE Codex payload (`payloads.<triple>.{path,sha256}`) plus npm `dist.integrity` provenance for the platform package. This replaces `.claude/governance/codex-cli-binary-sha256.txt`, which is retired as a comment-only tombstone: its `shasum -a 256 $(which codex)` procedure hashed the npm JS launcher, not the executing payload (see Vulnerability (b) above). Pin update ceremony (ADR-182 §Ceremony): after a legitimate upgrade the Owner runs `python3 .claude/hooks/check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin`, transcribes the reported payload `sha256` + `target_triple` into the manifest together with the registry `dist.integrity`, and lands the edit sentinel-gated (the manifest is canonical-guarded under `.claude/governance/*.json` AND kernel-enrolled in `_KERNEL_PATHS`).
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:362:**Asset:** Codex MCP request/response integrity. The Claude harness communicates with the Codex MCP server via a local stdio pipe (subprocess spawned by the MCP host). The stdio transport is the trust boundary between the Claude harness and the Codex process.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:370:**Vulnerability:** stdio is a plaintext local pipe with no integrity MAC or encryption. Any process running as the same UID can use `ptrace` (if not restricted by OS-level `ptrace_scope`) or `LD_PRELOAD` to intercept the stream. There is no in-band integrity check on the stdio bytes.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:536:| MITM on stdio transport (same-UID attacker) | No in-band integrity MAC (T-9) | Host-level UID isolation; OS ptrace_scope hardening |
